  "sections": [
    {
      "sectionCode": "book6-article-006-body",
      "heading": "Reading the Book VI structure",
      "anchor": "article-body",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "The collapse of empires did not make identity and governance problems disappear when nation-state borders were redrawn. At the same time, the Russian Revolution established a new direction of political and economic organization, while the Soviet runtime turned revolutionary institutional design into a longer-lived state structure."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "This reading places territorial and institutional reconfiguration side by side. Borders can change quickly, while institutional reproduction, economic coordination and social legitimacy operate on longer timescales."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "This article distinguishes historical fact, historical reconstruction and structural interpretation in the manuscript. Later outcomes are not treated as inevitable at the time, and motives or causal claims not present in the manuscript are not added."
        }
      ]
    }
